  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 820 PM MST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nThe coming week looks to be mostly quiet for eastern Utah and \nWestern Colorado with temperatures four to eight degrees above \nnormal. A weak ridge/high  builds in over the Southwest extending to \nthe northeast across the region with a zonal flow aloft to our \nnorth. A weak shortwave in that flow will brush the northern \nmountains Tuesday into Wednesday that could kick off a few light \norographic showers/flurries, but don't expect any impacts with \nthis system. With the zonal flow dipping south, we could see \nsome afternoon and evening west to northwest winds gusting 20-30\nmph across the northern areas. Wednesday into the weekend will \nsee high pressure build in over the region with a return of mild\nweather. Models are indicating the next significant weather \nwe'll see is a cold low dropping south off the West Coast this \nweekend that will pull subtropical moisture north into the \nregion Sunday into early next week. It's too early to tell how \nmuch moisture will get caught up in this system, but current \nmodels are predicting pwats around 0.70 inches which is enough \nto give us a round of stronger thunderstorms, but not the deep \nmoisture we've seen in the past few weeks that brings widespread\nflooding. Confidence is low this far out, so stay tuned, but it\ndoes look like this system will usher in a period of more \nunsettled weather next week.",
